we are going to be speaking with  stephanie kelton who's a professor of  public policy and economics at Stony  Brook University and one of the world's  leading experts in modern monetary  theory unfortunately due to a technical  issue as I would call it we lost the  first bit of the conversation with her  so I'm introducing her to you now and  we're going to join the conversation in  progress if if Mitch McConnell and the  Republicans have decided that you know  the four bills that have been passed so  far that's enough that's sufficient  we're going to close up and go home  we're not going to do anything that's  going to add to the deficit further  we're beginning to worry about the debt  and you're gonna leave state and local  governments out to dry you're gonna you  know there's a number of other things  but I have to tell you that's the one  that concerns me the most because you're  already hearing governors tell you  they're very candid about what the  consequences will be if they don't get  aid from the federal government they're  going to start cutting the public  workforce firefighters teachers police  officers essential services everything  is going to be cut and it's going to be  cut deeply they're going to be 20% 30%  cuts more layoffs and so what Congress  is trying to do to put some kind of a  floor under the slowdown and you know to  provide some support for this economy is  going to be completely undone when you  get 50 little herbert hoover's running  around all over this country working  with their state legislatures to slash  budgets so now along those lines we the  unemployment insurance system these are  state systems which are being there  independently operated some you know you  file by phone some website the systems  are sort of functioning to different  degrees depending on how overwhelmed and  modern they are we've got the federal  supplements for unemployment insurance  of up to $600 a week we're starting to  hear that there are state governors  saying we can only really maintain the  state benefits for so long before those  run out as they say what is your  perspective on what needs to be done in  that sense well a couple of things the  the mistakes have already been made in  the sense that once you allow people to  become  unemployed then the way that you're left  to deal with them is through  unemployment insurance and the the thing  that happened after the Great Recession  you probably remember is that people who  went on unemployment insurance then have  the unemployment insurance that period  of time runs out and then it expires and  now you don't have anything and then you  have to go back and Congress has to  fight and negotiate and pass a small  extension and then it turns out not to  be long enough and you got all these  people who became long-term unemployed  26 weeks and more many people were  jobless for a year or more in that  environment and the benefits run out and  all of the associated problems so it  would have been I just want to say it  would have been much much better to have  an effective paycheck protection program  in place that allowed employers to keep  hold of their workers keep them on  payroll avoid letting them slide into  unemployment and then have this system  which as you described is antiquated  you've got people who are reporting that  they've made a thousand phone calls a  day to try to apply to get in the queue  for unemployment insurance so you know  what happens when it runs out well I  guess we we hope for the best in terms  of Congress being willing to extend  unemployment insurance as needed or  people are just going to be left with  with no income at all and that's  fundamentally I mean that's how it would  have to be managed right it would have  to be managed where the Treasury says we  are going to fund these state programs  or we will make up anything that the  states are unable to pay that's the  mechanism right and that's we've just  been just yet that's exactly right  if what you have a situation where the  federal government sits in a unique  position as the issuer of the currency  it can't run out of money state and  local governments completely different  state local governments can't issue the  US dollar they are users of the currency  which is why every mayor and every  governor essentially is pleading with  the federal government to come through  with grants with aid to help state and  local governments because their budgets  don't work like the federal budget and  so that's exactly right and  where revenues are collapsing at the  state and local level and state local  governments are dependent on tax revenue  to pay the bills whereas the federal  government isn't the federal government  is going to have to step up and write  the cheque or we're going to end up in a  situation where you have mass layoffs  across the country state public sector  workers and essentially states that are  unable to bear the burden of supporting  the unemployment insurance system I read  a paper yesterday that argues that this  is actually going to be much worse and  last much longer than certainly the  administration is willing to admit when  they say it's going to go up like a  rocket analogies to Great Depression  number two my question to you is what  are the indicators we should be paying  attention to to determine whether that's  the direction we're going or whether in  six or eight months there could be a  significant portion of the losses  recovered I mean I think we're watching  the unemployment right I think that you  know you you can extend loans and say  that they turn into grants but you're  still we are still relying on businesses  to apply for these loans to take some  risk because it doesn't cover all of  their fixed operating costs we're  essentially saying you know do us a  favor help us keep people on payroll but  we're gonna cover some of your fixed  costs but not all of your fixed costs  through this profit go through this  process so businesses that have let a  lease they have rent they have utilities  they have other fixed costs that they  can't cover because they're shut down  they don't have any cash flow right they  have no revenue they're not going to  take those loans and keep the workers on  payroll if it means they also have to  incur a bunch of other costs that won't  be covered that's going to come out of  their bottom line so watch the  unemployment rate and watch how many of  these businesses are essentially going  to be gone forever and that's the really  scary thing as small businesses really  are the backbone of our economy in terms  of you know supporting jobs and you know  I talked with people I got a friend out  in Kansas City and he said  a few weeks ago you could drive around  and you can see signs in the business  storefronts that said temporarily closed  right he went back a few days ago and he  said the signs no longer say that they  just say out of business you know  they're boarding up they're going under  so I think those are the kind of  indicators that let you know that the  kind of thing that you just said that  this could be orders of magnitude worse  than the Great Recession and we could  end up with an economy that is deeply  depressed for a long long period of time  yeah here in Boston we've seen a shift  from open for takeout and delivery to  closed until further notice which is  sort of another sense of that couple  other things just to talk briefly about  before I let you go one is the stock  market this is looking at the level of  the Dow tell us anything relevant right  now well it tells you a little bit I  suppose it tells you that investors feel  less pay okay about the degree of  commitment on the part of the Fed to  help maintain stock market prices I mean  I think it's a reflection of not so much  you know fundamentals what we call  economic fundamentals the health of our  firms and the likelihood of a solid  recovery in the months ahead but sort of  confidence and a belief that at the end  of the day the Fed the Fed has your back  I guess the question is whether that  means anything for Main Street as they  like to say because at a quick glance  you see the unemployment numbers and you  see the stock market doing okay it  doesn't make sense now a lot of that is  how bad was it expected to get when  people go on unemployment they do  presumably get some money which they can  spend which could prop up stock market  there are other factors but how does it  relate to the average person's  experience and outlook not very directly  at all yeah I mean that's that's the  thing you know I think investors really  are mostly channeling optimism about how  far the Fed is going to be willing to go  to do whatever it  takes to protect the largest  publicly-traded companies and  shareholders last thing a universal  basic income there is a movement now so  universal basic income  I guess as prominent as it has ever been  probably mostly thanks to Andrew Yang's  candidacy in the Democratic primary  there are a lot of conversations now  which seem to go along the lines of hey  we clearly can afford to do what Andrew  yang suggested and we've clearly  determined it's a good idea we being I  guess you know our legislators lawmakers  others saying there's really nothing  here these are extenuating emergency  circumstances these are one-time  payments even if it's renewed one more  time this is not universal basic income  and it does not affect the economy in  the way that a ubi would what can we  glean about ubi from this if anything  well I mean the the twelve hundred  dollar check a lot of people are saying  just keep that going and that would be a  form of you know a basic income payment  again even with the twelve hundred  dollar check there is means-testing not  everybody is going to receive that check  with the yang proposal remember that you  know that proposal was tied to a whole  series of offends so if you want to opt  into this program then check the math  and make sure that if you're receiving  other forms of public support if you're  on food stamps or you know yeah there  were a range of public programs that if  you're a beneficiary you need to decide  whether you want to continue to receive  those benefits or let those benefits go  and take essentially what's behind door  number two with although I think later  on it became changed to be additive  although I don't know I'm not  remembering no no no no it was additive  in in the sense that if you're not on  any government program okay you get it  if you are on certain government  programs like Social Security  it was additive to that but it wasn't  additive to the whole package the whole  programs okay so but look a basic income  right now or in  I'm supplement to people makes perfect  sense to me makes perfect sense to me in  this moment that you need to get cash  into people's hands you got all these  cars you got thousands and thousands of  cars of people families lining up at the  food banks right people aren't at the  food bank because the grocery store is  out of food they're at the food bank  because the bank account is out of money  and so in in this situation direct  payments on a recurring basis for a  period of time make make perfect sense  to me you don't necessarily think this  tells us anything about the long-term  idea of ubi no not really I mean look if  we just did this conversation where I  said suppose we were in the fall of 2019  yes if we're in the fall of 2019 and we  were talking about sending every person  in this country which there is a  proposal Congress congresswoman Rasheeda  Talib has a proposal to send I think  it's $2,000 right every single person in  this country for a period of time now  that's a universal basic income payment  over a period of time  suppose we had talked about doing that  in the fall of last year when the  unemployment rate was three and a half  percent and I think that most people  would have said there's your inflation  invitation right because in that  economic environment people had jobs  people were already have you know had  income to spend and on top of that  you're gonna layer for 8,000 per  household depending on this number of  people in the house I think it's a very  different question in that economic  environment we've been speaking with  stephanie kelton professor of public  policy and economics at Stony Brook  University Stephanie really appreciate  your time once again thanks for having  me

